findings.claim:
The architecture rule guards spellings the file does not use and misses the one tag comparison that decides the NtfF column. Tag() compares a variable right-hand side: `$_.tag -ceq $name`. If that becomes `-eq $name` or `-ieq $name`, none of the three patterns match:
- pattern 1 needs `$WatchedTags`/`$NetTags` on the left;
- pattern 2 needs `-in $WatchedTags`;
- pattern 3 needs a quoted literal tag.
Also missed: the simplified syntax `Where-Object tag -eq $t`, a `switch` on `.tag` (case-insensitive by default, per about_Switch), and the raw-row form `$_[0] -eq ...` in Read-Mark. Once Ntff is in the marks, that `-eq` change returns whichever of NtfF/Ntff comes first. The invariant then holds only by remembering, at the site that matters.
